 # Integer-profile benchmark evidence
 
+This document has two parts. The **revision 2** section records the measurements
+behind the mixed-representation promotion matrix, shared storage, chunked
+storage, inherited budgets and run-aware Roaring construction. The **revision 1**
+section below it is the earlier report on the probe-direction change and remains
+the record for that revision's claims.
+
+## Revision 2: promotion, sharing, chunked storage
+
+### What changed and why it was measured
+
+The [adversarial review](reviews/integer-profile-adversarial-review.md) showed
+that every mixed-representation operation and every dense union beyond the window
+cap materialized a sorted array, that budgets did not survive construction, and
+that same-representation equality, Roaring construction and dense/Roaring pairs
+were far slower than the native kernels they wrapped. The revision promotes one
+operand into the other's representation (or edits a copy of the larger one),
+shares storage behind an `Arc`, adds chunked storage with per-container sharing,
+carries budgets into results, builds Roaring with bulk appends and range inserts,
+and compares same representations natively.
+
+### Reproduction and evidence
+
+All records are under [`verification/int-set/revision2/`](../verification/int-set/revision2/README.md).
+Two instruments were used:
+
+1. The repository benchmark, `cargo bench --locked --features int-set,roaring-0_12 --bench integer_sets`,
+   run three times on the revised crate (`bench-1..3.csv`) and three times on
+   the 0.4.0 binary, interleaved (`baseline-binary-1..3.csv`). **Unchanged control
+   loops moved by up to 2× between the two binaries** (`u32.cluster.btree.intersection_count`
+   measured 24–37 µs in the baseline binary and 54–77 µs in the revised one, with
+   `std::BTreeSet` code that neither revision touches). The pair is kept as a record
+   of that code-placement effect; it is not the basis of any ratio below.
+2. A same-binary A/B harness (`ab-harness/`) that links the 0.4.0 integer profile
+   (renamed `frost_baseline`) and the revised crate into one executable, validates
+   every answer against `BTreeSet`, and measures old and new alternately for three
+   rounds of five samples (≥ 4 ms batches). `ab-same-binary.csv` reports the minimum
+   per-round median for each version and the representation each produced. **All
+   ratios below come from this file.** 413 cases are paired; 214 are new (chunked
+   storage, mixed union/difference, snapshot, clone, equality).
+
+Host: shared 2-vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.10 GHz VM, Rust 1.99.1, Cargo release defaults,
+`RUSTFLAGS` unset (x86-64 baseline, so dense popcounts are software sequences),
+`taskset -c 1`. Timing variance between rounds reached 1.5× on this host; ratios
+between about 0.75× and 1.3× on sub-microsecond cases are not evidence of change.
+Of the 413 paired cases, 187 were at least 1.3× faster, 166 within ±10 %, and 31
+at least 13 % slower; the slower set changed composition between runs except for
+the cases discussed under "Slower observations".
+
+### The review's reproducers
+
+| Case | 0.4.0 | Revised | Ratio | Representation |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| 1 M-member dense ∪ Roaring singleton | 2,683 µs | 32.4 µs | 83× | Sorted (7.8 MiB) → Dense (244 KiB) |
+| 1 M-member dense ∖ sorted{4} | 3,372 µs | 29.0 µs | 116× | Sorted → Dense |
+| Two 1 M-member dense islands 2^27 apart, ∪ | 7,905 µs | 67.0 µs | 118× | Sorted (16 MiB) → Roaring (≈ 4 MiB estimate, 1.8 KB serialized) |
+| `==`, 200 K members, Roaring vs Roaring | 3,102 µs | 1.95 µs | 1,587× | native container compare |
+| `==`, 200 K members, Dense vs Dense | 946 µs | 1.24 µs | 764× | window compare |
+| `==`, 200 K members, Sorted vs Sorted | 1,453 µs | 27.3 µs | 53× | slice compare |
+| build 200 K members, Roaring | 2,662 µs | 1,441 µs | 1.8× | bulk append |
+| 1,000-member run, `contains` ×1,000 (auto) | 3.43 µs | 1.94 µs | 1.8× | Roaring → Dense (floor) |
+
+### Mixed pairs on the u64 large-base fixture (8,192 vs 5,462 members)
+
+| Left / right | Operation | 0.4.0 | Revised | Ratio | Result |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| Dense / Roaring | count | 48.7 µs | 1.42 µs | 34× | promoted, native |
+| Roaring / Dense | count | 23.5 µs | 1.32 µs | 18× | promoted, native |
+| Dense / Roaring | ∩ materialize | 81.3 µs | 8.41 µs | 9.7× | Sorted → Roaring |
+| Dense / Roaring | ∪ materialize | 76.5 µs | 2.01 µs | 38× | Sorted → Roaring |
+| Roaring / Dense | ∖ materialize | 76.1 µs | 1.93 µs | 39× | Sorted → Roaring |
+| Dense / Sorted | ∖ materialize | 142.5 µs | 11.6 µs | 12× | Sorted → Dense (copy and clear) |
+| Dense / Sorted | ∪ materialize | 41.3 µs | 11.5 µs | 3.6× | Sorted → Dense (grown window) |
+| Roaring / Sorted | ∪ materialize | 94.5 µs | 42.6 µs | 2.2× | Sorted → Roaring (promoted) |
+| Sorted / Roaring | count | 36.5 µs | 34.2 µs | 1.1× | probing, unchanged |
+
+On the skewed u32 fixture (65,536 vs 64), a Roaring ∪ sorted{64} went from
+512 µs (sorted merge of every member) to 0.37 µs (copy and 64 inserts, 1,376×),
+and a dense ∖ Roaring{64} from 645 µs to 1.37 µs (471×). Where the sorted operand
+dominates (sorted{65,536} ∪ Roaring{64}; the scattered-prefix fixture), results
+stay sorted and the times are within noise of the old merge (0.7–1.3×).
+
+### Dense kernels, sharing and the snapshot pattern
+
+| Case | 0.4.0 | Revised | Ratio |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Dense ∩ Dense materialize, large-base | 795 ns | 331 ns | 2.4× |
+| Dense ∪ Dense materialize, large-base | 1,006 ns | 346 ns | 2.9× |
+| Dense ∖ Dense materialize, runs | 8.79 µs | 3.49 µs | 2.5× |
+| `clone`, sorted 8,192 members | 1,644 ns | 17 ns | 97× |
+| `clone`, Roaring, scattered prefixes | 207 µs | 17 ns | 12,161× |
+| `==`, sorted 8,192 vs equal twin | 46.4 µs | 1.54 µs | 30× |
+| 64 singleton unions, large-base, Roaring | 911 µs | 47.5 µs | 19× |
+| 64 singleton unions, large-base, chunked | — | 32.0 µs | new |
+| 64 singleton unions, u32 window, auto (Roaring) | 7,005 µs | 11.2 µs | 625× |
+| 64 singleton unions, u32 window, dense | 6,362 µs | 2,028 µs | 3.1× |
+| 64 singleton unions, u32 window, chunked | — | 26.1 µs | new |
+| 64 singleton unions, large-base, dense | 942 µs | 1,244 µs | 0.76× |
+
+The last row is a documented trade-off: the dense result now grows its window
+to cover the far-away additions (127 KiB, within twice the sorted payload) so
+membership stays one load, where 0.4.0 produced a 65 KiB sorted array. Chunked
+storage is the representation for this pattern: it copies one container and the
+key index per update and shares the rest (`shared_chunks` reports it).
+
+Construction: Roaring candidates build 1.5–10.7× faster across fixtures (bulk
+`append` for sparse stretches, `insert_range` for runs of ≥ 32; the four-run
+fixture went from 137 µs to 12.8 µs) and automatic construction 1.2–6.6×. Sorted
+and dense construction, whose code is unchanged, measured 0.6–1.1× across runs
+of the harness; an isolated interleaved probe (`review-probe`, `construct`) put
+old and new within round-to-round variance of each other.
+
+### Chunked storage
+
+On the large-base fixture, chunked ∩ counts in 0.87 µs (Roaring 1.27 µs),
+materializes ∩ in 4.4 µs (Roaring ≈ 4 µs) and ∪ in 1.8 µs (Roaring 1.4 µs); its
+snapshot updates are the fastest of any representation. On the scattered-prefix
+fixture it is the slowest candidate (4,096 containers of one member each:
+construction 603 µs, 64 snapshot updates 4.99 ms versus 0.49 ms for sorted). The
+planner therefore never selects it; it is an explicit choice for clustered data
+with many derived versions.
+
+### Slower observations
+
+Besides the dense snapshot row above, these cases were slower in more than one
+run and are explained by the design rather than by noise:
+
+- Operations on sets of a few members pay one extra pointer dereference for the
+  shared `Arc`; the same-representation count paths measured 0.9–1.0× on this
+  host with excursions to 0.75× that did not reproduce between runs.
+- Merges whose sorted operand dominates a scattered Roaring treemap (the
+  scattered-prefix fixture) measured 0.7–0.75× in two of three runs. The code
+  path (merge into sorted codes) is the same as in 0.4.0; the treemap iteration
+  dominates both.
+
+Cases that appeared below 0.87× in one run and above 0.9× in another (sorted and
+dense construction, `u32.runs` Roaring operators, `u32.skew` dense/sorted counts)
+are recorded in the CSV and not claimed either way.
+
+### Scope of the result
+
+The A/B figures hold for the listed fixtures on this host. They do not measure
+resident memory (payload figures are estimates from cardinality and window size),
+allocation counts, cache-cold behavior, other targets or `target-cpu` levels. The
+dense kernels remain popcount-bound at the x86-64 baseline; enabling POPCNT
+(`-C target-cpu=x86-64-v2` or higher) halved dense counts in the review's probe.
+
+## Revision 1: probe direction (historical)
+
 The measurements led to one targeted implementation change: mixed intersections
 now compare scan and lookup work before choosing a probe direction. On the
 large-base fixture, Sorted/Dense intersection counts improved by **10.9 / 10.7 times**, and Sorted/Roaring counts by **4.0 / 4.0 times**, across two matched runs.
